Subject: RegExp: anchored attempts around each required-literal occurrence, match result elements in the object's block

What this does
  - quickjs.c: when the analysis of a pattern (0532/0533) gives a required literal that a match must start at most 8 code units before (non-Unicode patterns), the generic and first-set strategies no longer enter the scanning matcher after the first occurrence; they find each occurrence in turn and run the matcher anchored (`lre_exec_at`) at the few possible starts before it, in increasing order, so the subject between occurrences is never scanned and a failed occurrence moves on to the next.
  - `js_regexp_exec` builds the match result array with `JS_NewObjectFromShapeCapExtra`, so the captures live in the array's own block (one allocation instead of two); `js_regexp_result_prealloc` is removed.

Why we need it
  Post-0533 census: the pattern with a required literal after a group still cost 7,025 cycles per scan because the occurrence of the literal that did not complete a match let the scanner run on to the end of the subject. Every exec call allocated the result object and its element storage separately.

Soundness
  Any match starting at s contains the literal at some occurrence L with s <= L <= s + prefix_max; occurrences are visited in increasing order and the starts of one occurrence in increasing order, skipping starts already tried, so the first success is the leftmost match. The Unicode flags are excluded because positions there are not code units.

Failure modes
  A pattern with a frequent literal and a bounded prefix tries up to prefix_max + 1 anchored starts per occurrence.

